I think now would be a good time to reiterate an applicable detail I
mentioned in the first post concerning this patch:
>* Gray dragon corpses now confer half magic resistance when eaten.

So, the importance and rarity of magic resistance in this patch is now
in the league of cold and shock resistance, which I think is fair
considering its main function is resisting magic missiles.

>Anyway,
>'magic' >= 'spell', so I think semantically and logically you're pretty
>free to do whatever.

Magic resistance in my patch is not "anything magic-related"
resistance, it is "destructive mana energy" resistance. I tried to make
that clear earlier.

>Calling a level teleporter a spell seems odd, yet a wizard's
>ricocheting MM spell will ignore his own 'spell' resistance.

Well, duh. A wizard's ricocheting cone of cold spell will ignore his
spell resistance as well. All the "magic missile" spell does is create
a ray of mana and fire it. The rest is consequential.

Okay, I suppose you're wondering about the "force bolt" spell, then. In
that case, I will remove spell resistance's ability to nullify it.
It'll put an end to those unhelpful "Boing!" messages, for one thing.

>Its still completely counter intuitive that being able to take
>*more* punishment and surive means that some attack does
>*more* damage to you.

It doesn't do _more_ damage, it just takes away more hit points. Big
difference.



